                                        "entry_id": "4e0e8fbf-84a4-388d-80b7-2c3a74b9d712",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The outer (lockable) door of a set of rooms in a college or similar institution. (Often in the phrase sport one's oak.)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e23edc3a-24a8-4f72-b8d4-17bceaf5374e",
                                                "sense_id": "eb8364cb-58cc-3818-8621-1fd56dd1be33",
                                                "text": "[E]very set of rooms has two doors, and I soon learned that the outer door, which is thick and solid, is called the oak, and to shut it is termed, to sport."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7d0d2960-ee6b-4f1d-ace2-76fb41871916",
                                                "sense_id": "eb8364cb-58cc-3818-8621-1fd56dd1be33",
                                                "text": "It was hardly the thing for a master to sport his oak where another member of the staff was concerned."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0adf9be2-94a4-4d73-9864-ac3f7f65faad",
                                                "sense_id": "eb8364cb-58cc-3818-8621-1fd56dd1be33",
                                                "text": "The vesper bell had rung its parting note; the domini were mostly caged in comfortable quarters, discussing the merits of old port; and the merry student had closed his oak, to consecrate the night to friendship, sack, and claret."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
